const a=JSON.parse(`{"work":{"id":"Persuasion","title":"Persuasion","author":"Jane Austen","paragraph_count":1011,"chapter_count":24,"scene_count":139},"summary_basis":{"source_of_truth_for_mapping":"persuasion.json","summary_and_analysis_source":"persuasion_sparknotes_summaries_and_analysis(1).json","note":"Chapter/paragraph/scene mapping is taken from persuasion.json. Chapter summaries and paired-chapter analyses are taken from the newer SparkNotes summaries-and-analysis JSON."},"mapping":{"paragraph_id_field":"paragraphs[].id","chapter_id_field":"paragraphs[].chapter","scene_id_field":"paragraphs[].scene","location_field":"paragraphs[].location","recommended_chapter_tooltip_field":"chapters[].tooltip_summary","recommended_chapter_analysis_field":"chapters[].analysis","analysis_group_lookup_field":"analysis_groups[].analysis_id"},"chapters":[{"chapter_id":"C01","chapter_number":1,"source_chapter_id":"ch01","paragraph_start":"P1","paragraph_end":"P23","paragraph_count":23,"scene_ids":["S1"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"],"tooltip_summary":"Sir Walter Elliot's extravagant spending has put the Elliot family under financial pressure. The chapter introduces his daughters Elizabeth, Anne, and Mary, the death of Lady Elliot, and Lady Russell's continuing role as a trusted family adviser.","summary":{"short":"Sir Walter Elliot's extravagant spending has put the Elliot family under financial pressure. The chapter introduces his daughters Elizabeth, Anne, and Mary, the death of Lady Elliot, and Lady Russell's continuing role as a trusted family adviser.","detailed":"At Kellynch Hall, Sir Walter is established as vain, status-conscious, and absorbed in the Baronetage and his family rank. The three Elliot daughters are contrasted: Elizabeth resembles her father, Anne is sensible but undervalued, and Mary is married to Charles Musgrove. Because there is no son, the estate will eventually pass to their cousin William Elliot, who has long been estranged from the family. Sir Walter's excessive spending has created serious debt, forcing Lady Russell and Mr. Shepherd to consider how the household can reduce expenses."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch01_02","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 1 & 2","analysis":"SparkNotes reads the opening chapters as establishing both the Elliot family's financial crisis and the social pressures behind it. Sir Walter's need to preserve the appearance of aristocratic status makes retrenchment difficult, while the decision to move to Bath demonstrates persuasion working through his vanity rather than through practical reasoning. The analysis also treats Sir Walter as a foil to Anne: his vanity, image-consciousness, and poor judgment highlight her humility, sensibility, and practical intelligence.","analysis_chapters":[1,2],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S1","chapter_id":"C01","paragraph_start":"P1","paragraph_end":"P23","paragraph_count":23,"location":"Kellynch Hall","dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section1/"}},{"chapter_id":"C02","chapter_number":2,"source_chapter_id":"ch02","paragraph_start":"P24","paragraph_end":"P40","paragraph_count":17,"scene_ids":["S2"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"],"tooltip_summary":"Sir Walter resists serious cuts to his lifestyle but eventually accepts Mr. Shepherd's proposal that the family leave Kellynch and live more cheaply in Bath, provided a suitable tenant can be found.","summary":{"short":"Sir Walter resists serious cuts to his lifestyle but eventually accepts Mr. Shepherd's proposal that the family leave Kellynch and live more cheaply in Bath, provided a suitable tenant can be found.","detailed":"Lady Russell and Mr. Shepherd propose retrenchment, but Sir Walter refuses reductions that he believes would diminish his social standing. Anne favors stronger economies, yet her advice carries little weight. Mr. Shepherd therefore suggests temporarily leaving Kellynch Hall, which would make reduced spending less conspicuous. Sir Walter agrees to relocate to Bath if a respectable tenant can rent the estate. Lady Russell also welcomes the move because she hopes it will separate Elizabeth from Mrs. Clay, whose growing intimacy with the family she distrusts."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch01_02","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 1 & 2","analysis":"SparkNotes reads the opening chapters as establishing both the Elliot family's financial crisis and the social pressures behind it. Sir Walter's need to preserve the appearance of aristocratic status makes retrenchment difficult, while the decision to move to Bath demonstrates persuasion working through his vanity rather than through practical reasoning. The analysis also treats Sir Walter as a foil to Anne: his vanity, image-consciousness, and poor judgment highlight her humility, sensibility, and practical intelligence.","analysis_chapters":[1,2],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S2","chapter_id":"C02","paragraph_start":"P24","paragraph_end":"P40","paragraph_count":17,"location":"Kellynch Hall","dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section1/"}},{"chapter_id":"C03","chapter_number":3,"source_chapter_id":"ch03","paragraph_start":"P41","paragraph_end":"P78","paragraph_count":38,"scene_ids":["S3"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"],"tooltip_summary":"The Elliots discuss possible tenants for Kellynch, and Admiral Croft emerges as an acceptable candidate despite Sir Walter's prejudices about naval officers.","summary":{"short":"The Elliots discuss possible tenants for Kellynch, and Admiral Croft emerges as an acceptable candidate despite Sir Walter's prejudices about naval officers.","detailed":"With peace returning naval officers to shore, Mr. Shepherd argues that a sailor would make a careful and financially secure tenant. The conversation exposes Sir Walter's contempt for the Navy, which he believes allows men of humble birth to rise socially and damages their appearance. Anne defends naval service. Admiral Croft, a wealthy and respectable officer with local connections, is proposed as tenant. Once reassured about the Admiral's appearance and status, Sir Walter approves him. Anne realizes that the Crofts' arrival may also bring Captain Wentworth back into her vicinity."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch03_04","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 3 & 4","analysis":"The analysis emphasizes the Navy as a vehicle of social mobility that conflicts with Sir Walter's attachment to inherited rank. Anne values naval service, while Sir Walter resents the ability of officers of obscure birth to gain wealth and status. Chapter 4 develops the novel's central concern with persuasion through Anne's broken engagement: she yields to older advisers despite her own feelings, leaving open the question of when persuadability is prudent and when it becomes a weakness. SparkNotes also notes Austen's use of free indirect discourse to blend a character's viewpoint with the narrator's voice.","analysis_chapters":[3,4],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S3","chapter_id":"C03","paragraph_start":"P41","paragraph_end":"P78","paragraph_count":38,"location":"Kellynch Hall","dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section2/"}},{"chapter_id":"C04","chapter_number":4,"source_chapter_id":"ch04","paragraph_start":"P79","paragraph_end":"P90","paragraph_count":12,"scene_ids":["S4"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Kellynch and Uppercross"],"tooltip_summary":"The narrative reveals Anne's earlier engagement to Captain Frederick Wentworth, which she ended after Lady Russell and her family persuaded her that the match was imprudent.","summary":{"short":"The narrative reveals Anne's earlier engagement to Captain Frederick Wentworth, which she ended after Lady Russell and her family persuaded her that the match was imprudent.","detailed":"A flashback recounts Anne and Wentworth's romance eight years earlier. Wentworth, then a young naval officer without fortune or high social rank, fell deeply in love with Anne and wanted to marry her. Sir Walter disapproved, and Lady Russell believed the match beneath Anne and too uncertain financially. Young and deferential, Anne accepted Lady Russell's advice and broke the engagement, believing self-denial might ultimately benefit Wentworth as well. Wentworth left angry and wounded. Although he later gained wealth and professional success, Anne has continued to regret the loss and has not emotionally forgotten him."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch03_04","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 3 & 4","analysis":"The analysis emphasizes the Navy as a vehicle of social mobility that conflicts with Sir Walter's attachment to inherited rank. Anne values naval service, while Sir Walter resents the ability of officers of obscure birth to gain wealth and status. Chapter 4 develops the novel's central concern with persuasion through Anne's broken engagement: she yields to older advisers despite her own feelings, leaving open the question of when persuadability is prudent and when it becomes a weakness. SparkNotes also notes Austen's use of free indirect discourse to blend a character's viewpoint with the narrator's voice.","analysis_chapters":[3,4],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S4","chapter_id":"C04","paragraph_start":"P79","paragraph_end":"P90","paragraph_count":12,"location":"Kellynch and Uppercross","dataset_locations":["Kellynch and Uppercross"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section2/"}},{"chapter_id":"C05","chapter_number":5,"source_chapter_id":"ch05","paragraph_start":"P91","paragraph_end":"P136","paragraph_count":46,"scene_ids":["S5","S6","S7","S8","S9","S10","S11","S12","S13","S14","S15","S16","S17","S18"],"scene_count":14,"dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall","Uppercross Cottage","Uppercross Great House"],"tooltip_summary":"The Crofts formally agree to rent Kellynch. The Elliot household prepares for Bath, while Anne chooses first to stay with Mary at Uppercross.","summary":{"short":"The Crofts formally agree to rent Kellynch. The Elliot household prepares for Bath, while Anne chooses first to stay with Mary at Uppercross.","detailed":"Admiral and Mrs. Croft inspect Kellynch and quickly reach a rental agreement with Sir Walter. Sir Walter and Elizabeth plan to take Mrs. Clay with them to Bath, which worries Anne and Lady Russell because they fear Mrs. Clay may become too close to Sir Walter. Mary asks Anne to stay with her at Uppercross Cottage before going to Bath, and Anne gladly accepts. At Uppercross, Anne finds Mary complaining of illness and domestic frustrations, and she is introduced more fully to the lively, affectionate Musgrove family at the Great House."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch05_06","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 5 & 6","analysis":"SparkNotes uses Anne's movement between the Elliot and Musgrove households to examine class distinctions. The Musgroves are wealthy landowners but untitled, and their domestic interests contrast with the Elliots' fixation on rank and appearance. Anne appreciates their warmth without idealizing their lack of education or elegance. The analysis also focuses on women's precarious social position within the class system: marriage can rapidly raise or lower a woman's status, which helps explain the family's anxiety about Mrs. Clay and the importance placed on choosing a socially appropriate husband.","analysis_chapters":[5,6],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S5","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P91","paragraph_end":"P96","paragraph_count":6,"location":"Kellynch Hall","dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"]},{"scene_id":"S6","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P97","paragraph_end":"P97","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Cottage","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage"]},{"scene_id":"S7","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P98","paragraph_end":"P99","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Kellynch Hall","dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"]},{"scene_id":"S8","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P100","paragraph_end":"P100","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Cottage","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage"]},{"scene_id":"S9","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P101","paragraph_end":"P108","paragraph_count":8,"location":"Kellynch Hall","dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"]},{"scene_id":"S10","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P109","paragraph_end":"P110","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Uppercross Cottage","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage"]},{"scene_id":"S11","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P111","paragraph_end":"P117","paragraph_count":7,"location":"Kellynch Hall","dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"]},{"scene_id":"S12","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P118","paragraph_end":"P118","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S13","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P119","paragraph_end":"P130","paragraph_count":12,"location":"Kellynch Hall","dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"]},{"scene_id":"S14","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P131","paragraph_end":"P131","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S15","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P132","paragraph_end":"P133","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Kellynch Hall","dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"]},{"scene_id":"S16","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P134","paragraph_end":"P134","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S17","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P135","paragraph_end":"P135","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Kellynch Hall","dataset_locations":["Kellynch Hall"]},{"scene_id":"S18","chapter_id":"C05","paragraph_start":"P136","paragraph_end":"P136","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section3/"}},{"chapter_id":"C06","chapter_number":6,"source_chapter_id":"ch06","paragraph_start":"P137","paragraph_end":"P170","paragraph_count":34,"scene_ids":["S19","S20","S21","S22","S23","S24","S25","S26","S27"],"scene_count":9,"dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage and Great House","Uppercross Great House"],"tooltip_summary":"Anne settles into Uppercross life, where the Musgroves' interests and family atmosphere contrast sharply with the Elliots. She learns that Captain Wentworth will soon arrive.","summary":{"short":"Anne settles into Uppercross life, where the Musgroves' interests and family atmosphere contrast sharply with the Elliots. She learns that Captain Wentworth will soon arrive.","detailed":"Anne finds Uppercross more cheerful and practical than Kellynch, although she is repeatedly drawn into small disagreements between Mary, Charles, and the elder Musgroves. Charles and Mary's marriage is workable but strained by Mary's moods and complaints, and Anne becomes a useful mediator within the family. Her nephews respond well to her calm authority. During visits involving the Crofts and Kellynch, the family learns that Mrs. Croft's brother, Captain Wentworth, is expected soon. His impending arrival makes Anne acutely aware that she may soon have to face him again."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch05_06","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 5 & 6","analysis":"SparkNotes uses Anne's movement between the Elliot and Musgrove households to examine class distinctions. The Musgroves are wealthy landowners but untitled, and their domestic interests contrast with the Elliots' fixation on rank and appearance. Anne appreciates their warmth without idealizing their lack of education or elegance. The analysis also focuses on women's precarious social position within the class system: marriage can rapidly raise or lower a woman's status, which helps explain the family's anxiety about Mrs. Clay and the importance placed on choosing a socially appropriate husband.","analysis_chapters":[5,6],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S19","chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P137","paragraph_end":"P143","paragraph_count":7,"location":"Uppercross Cottage and Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage and Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S20","chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P144","paragraph_end":"P144","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S21","chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P145","paragraph_end":"P145","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Cottage and Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage and Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S22","chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P146","paragraph_end":"P146","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S23","chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P147","paragraph_end":"P149","paragraph_count":3,"location":"Uppercross Cottage and Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage and Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S24","chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P150","paragraph_end":"P150","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S25","chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P151","paragraph_end":"P161","paragraph_count":11,"location":"Uppercross Cottage and Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage and Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S26","chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P162","paragraph_end":"P162","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S27","chapter_id":"C06","paragraph_start":"P163","paragraph_end":"P170","paragraph_count":8,"location":"Uppercross Cottage and Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage and Great House"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section3/"}},{"chapter_id":"C07","chapter_number":7,"source_chapter_id":"ch07","paragraph_start":"P171","paragraph_end":"P211","paragraph_count":41,"scene_ids":["S28","S29","S30","S31","S32"],"scene_count":5,"dataset_locations":["Uppercross","Uppercross Great House"],"tooltip_summary":"Wentworth arrives and begins visiting the Musgroves. Anne twice misses meeting him because she remains with Mary's injured son, then finally sees him briefly and learns he has criticized her changed appearance.","summary":{"short":"Wentworth arrives and begins visiting the Musgroves. Anne twice misses meeting him because she remains with Mary's injured son, then finally sees him briefly and learns he has criticized her changed appearance.","detailed":"Captain Wentworth comes to Kellynch and quickly becomes popular with the Musgroves. Anne is nervous about seeing him after eight years, but Mary's son suffers a serious fall just as Anne and Mary are preparing to visit the Great House. Anne stays to help care for the child while others socialize with Wentworth. When she finally encounters him, the meeting is brief and painful. Mary later reports that Wentworth thought Anne much altered. The narration also reveals that Wentworth still resents Anne's earlier rejection and interprets it as weakness of character; he now imagines marrying someone else."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch07_08","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 7 & 8","analysis":"The analysis highlights Austen's free indirect discourse, especially as the narration tracks Anne's intense response to seeing Wentworth again without simply quoting her thoughts. It also compares different performances of motherhood. Mary's concern for her injured son is presented as shallow and self-centered, whereas Mrs. Musgrove's grief for her late son is more genuine, though it too creates a social claim on Wentworth's attention. These contrasts show Austen's interest in how people inhabit family and social roles.","analysis_chapters":[7,8],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S28","chapter_id":"C07","paragraph_start":"P171","paragraph_end":"P171","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross","dataset_locations":["Uppercross"]},{"scene_id":"S29","chapter_id":"C07","paragraph_start":"P172","paragraph_end":"P172","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S30","chapter_id":"C07","paragraph_start":"P173","paragraph_end":"P191","paragraph_count":19,"location":"Uppercross","dataset_locations":["Uppercross"]},{"scene_id":"S31","chapter_id":"C07","paragraph_start":"P192","paragraph_end":"P192","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S32","chapter_id":"C07","paragraph_start":"P193","paragraph_end":"P211","paragraph_count":19,"location":"Uppercross","dataset_locations":["Uppercross"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section4/"}},{"chapter_id":"C08","chapter_number":8,"source_chapter_id":"ch08","paragraph_start":"P212","paragraph_end":"P270","paragraph_count":59,"scene_ids":["S33"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Uppercross"],"tooltip_summary":"Anne and Wentworth are now forced into the same social circle. At a Musgrove gathering, Wentworth discusses his naval life, comforts Mrs. Musgrove about her late son, and remains emotionally distant from Anne.","summary":{"short":"Anne and Wentworth are now forced into the same social circle. At a Musgrove gathering, Wentworth discusses his naval life, comforts Mrs. Musgrove about her late son, and remains emotionally distant from Anne.","detailed":"Repeated dinners and visits make Anne and Wentworth's proximity unavoidable, though they speak only when politeness requires it. Anne reflects painfully on how well suited they once were. During an evening with the Musgroves, Wentworth talks about his naval career and gives Mrs. Musgrove compassionate attention when she asks about her deceased son Dick, who served under him. The conversation also contrasts Wentworth's views about women at sea with Mrs. Croft's confidence in sharing her husband's naval life. Later, Anne plays music while others dance and socialize, leaving her emotionally isolated despite being physically present with the group."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch07_08","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 7 & 8","analysis":"The analysis highlights Austen's free indirect discourse, especially as the narration tracks Anne's intense response to seeing Wentworth again without simply quoting her thoughts. It also compares different performances of motherhood. Mary's concern for her injured son is presented as shallow and self-centered, whereas Mrs. Musgrove's grief for her late son is more genuine, though it too creates a social claim on Wentworth's attention. These contrasts show Austen's interest in how people inhabit family and social roles.","analysis_chapters":[7,8],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S33","chapter_id":"C08","paragraph_start":"P212","paragraph_end":"P270","paragraph_count":59,"location":"Uppercross","dataset_locations":["Uppercross"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section4/"}},{"chapter_id":"C09","chapter_number":9,"source_chapter_id":"ch09","paragraph_start":"P271","paragraph_end":"P304","paragraph_count":34,"scene_ids":["S34","S35","S36"],"scene_count":3,"dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage","Winthrop"],"tooltip_summary":"Charles Hayter returns and finds Henrietta increasingly interested in Wentworth. In a tense encounter at Uppercross, Wentworth quietly helps Anne by removing her troublesome nephew from her.","summary":{"short":"Charles Hayter returns and finds Henrietta increasingly interested in Wentworth. In a tense encounter at Uppercross, Wentworth quietly helps Anne by removing her troublesome nephew from her.","detailed":"Wentworth continues frequent visits to Uppercross, and speculation grows about whether he favors Henrietta or Louisa Musgrove. Charles Hayter, Henrietta's existing suitor, is disturbed by her changed attention. The chapter also emphasizes the social difference between the Hayter and Musgrove branches of the family and Mary's snobbery about the match. During one awkward morning, Wentworth unexpectedly finds Anne with an invalid child. When another young nephew clings to Anne and ignores Charles Hayter's attempts to stop him, Wentworth silently lifts the boy away. Anne is deeply affected by this small act of assistance but cannot comfortably acknowledge it."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch09_10","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 9 & 10","analysis":"SparkNotes treats Admiral and Mrs. Croft as one of Austen's clearest models of a successful long-term marriage. Their shared handling of the carriage becomes a metaphor for their partnership: responsibility shifts naturally between them as circumstances require. The analysis presents this relationship as unusually egalitarian for its historical context, challenging rigid divisions between male and female spheres and offering a model of the kind of companionate marriage the novel values.","analysis_chapters":[9,10],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S34","chapter_id":"C09","paragraph_start":"P271","paragraph_end":"P284","paragraph_count":14,"location":"Uppercross Cottage","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage"]},{"scene_id":"S35","chapter_id":"C09","paragraph_start":"P285","paragraph_end":"P285","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Winthrop","dataset_locations":["Winthrop"]},{"scene_id":"S36","chapter_id":"C09","paragraph_start":"P286","paragraph_end":"P304","paragraph_count":19,"location":"Uppercross Cottage","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Cottage"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section5/"}},{"chapter_id":"C10","chapter_number":10,"source_chapter_id":"ch10","paragraph_start":"P305","paragraph_end":"P352","paragraph_count":48,"scene_ids":["S37","S38","S39","S40","S41","S42","S43"],"scene_count":7,"dataset_locations":["Uppercross and Winthrop walk","Winthrop"],"tooltip_summary":"During a long walk near Uppercross, Louisa openly flirts with Wentworth and praises firmness of character. Wentworth learns that Charles Musgrove once proposed to Anne, and later makes sure a tired Anne gets a ride home.","summary":{"short":"During a long walk near Uppercross, Louisa openly flirts with Wentworth and praises firmness of character. Wentworth learns that Charles Musgrove once proposed to Anne, and later makes sure a tired Anne gets a ride home.","detailed":"Anne joins Mary, Charles, Henrietta, Louisa, and Wentworth on a country walk. Louisa repeatedly emphasizes her determination and suggests that true love should never yield to outside persuasion, ideas that resonate with Wentworth's judgment of Anne. The group passes the Hayters' home, where Henrietta reconnects with Charles Hayter despite Mary's social objections. Anne overhears Louisa telling Wentworth that Charles originally wanted to marry Anne before marrying Mary, information that clearly interests him. When Anne becomes tired, Wentworth discreetly arranges for Admiral and Mrs. Croft to take her home in their carriage, again showing unspoken concern."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch09_10","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 9 & 10","analysis":"SparkNotes treats Admiral and Mrs. Croft as one of Austen's clearest models of a successful long-term marriage. Their shared handling of the carriage becomes a metaphor for their partnership: responsibility shifts naturally between them as circumstances require. The analysis presents this relationship as unusually egalitarian for its historical context, challenging rigid divisions between male and female spheres and offering a model of the kind of companionate marriage the novel values.","analysis_chapters":[9,10],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S37","chapter_id":"C10","paragraph_start":"P305","paragraph_end":"P315","paragraph_count":11,"location":"Uppercross and Winthrop walk","dataset_locations":["Uppercross and Winthrop walk"]},{"scene_id":"S38","chapter_id":"C10","paragraph_start":"P316","paragraph_end":"P319","paragraph_count":4,"location":"Winthrop","dataset_locations":["Winthrop"]},{"scene_id":"S39","chapter_id":"C10","paragraph_start":"P320","paragraph_end":"P320","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross and Winthrop walk","dataset_locations":["Uppercross and Winthrop walk"]},{"scene_id":"S40","chapter_id":"C10","paragraph_start":"P321","paragraph_end":"P321","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Winthrop","dataset_locations":["Winthrop"]},{"scene_id":"S41","chapter_id":"C10","paragraph_start":"P322","paragraph_end":"P326","paragraph_count":5,"location":"Uppercross and Winthrop walk","dataset_locations":["Uppercross and Winthrop walk"]},{"scene_id":"S42","chapter_id":"C10","paragraph_start":"P327","paragraph_end":"P327","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Winthrop","dataset_locations":["Winthrop"]},{"scene_id":"S43","chapter_id":"C10","paragraph_start":"P328","paragraph_end":"P352","paragraph_count":25,"location":"Uppercross and Winthrop walk","dataset_locations":["Uppercross and Winthrop walk"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section5/"}},{"chapter_id":"C11","chapter_number":11,"source_chapter_id":"ch11","paragraph_start":"P353","paragraph_end":"P378","paragraph_count":26,"scene_ids":["S44","S45","S46"],"scene_count":3,"dataset_locations":["Uppercross","Uppercross Great House"],"tooltip_summary":"The Uppercross party travels to Lyme to visit Wentworth's naval friends, the Harvilles, and meets the grieving Captain Benwick, who finds Anne a sympathetic conversational partner.","summary":{"short":"The Uppercross party travels to Lyme to visit Wentworth's naval friends, the Harvilles, and meets the grieving Captain Benwick, who finds Anne a sympathetic conversational partner.","detailed":"Wentworth describes his friends in Lyme, prompting Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, and Louisa to join him on a trip there. The group is welcomed by Captain and Mrs. Harville, whose modest home impresses Anne because of its warmth, ingenuity, and hospitality. Captain Benwick, mourning his deceased fiancée Fanny Harville, is staying with them and has withdrawn into poetry and grief. Anne speaks with him thoughtfully about literature and sorrow, and he responds to her sympathy. The Lyme visit introduces a naval social world based more on affection, usefulness, and mutual support than on the rank-conscious values of Anne's family."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch11_12","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 11 & 12","analysis":"The analysis identifies Louisa's fall as the novel's major mid-point climax and notes that the original two-volume publication ended its first volume at Chapter 12, giving the accident the effect of a cliffhanger. The event also revises Wentworth's earlier admiration for firmness of character: Louisa's refusal to be persuaded becomes dangerous stubbornness, causing serious injury and forcing Wentworth to reconsider whether unwavering resolution is always a virtue.","analysis_chapters":[11,12],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S44","chapter_id":"C11","paragraph_start":"P353","paragraph_end":"P359","paragraph_count":7,"location":"Uppercross","dataset_locations":["Uppercross"]},{"scene_id":"S45","chapter_id":"C11","paragraph_start":"P360","paragraph_end":"P360","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Uppercross Great House","dataset_locations":["Uppercross Great House"]},{"scene_id":"S46","chapter_id":"C11","paragraph_start":"P361","paragraph_end":"P378","paragraph_count":18,"location":"Uppercross","dataset_locations":["Uppercross"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section6/"}},{"chapter_id":"C12","chapter_number":12,"source_chapter_id":"ch12","paragraph_start":"P379","paragraph_end":"P455","paragraph_count":77,"scene_ids":["S47","S48","S49","S50","S51","S52","S53","S54","S55","S56"],"scene_count":10,"dataset_locations":["Lyme Cobb and shore","Lyme Regis"],"tooltip_summary":"At Lyme, the group encounters Mr. Elliot, and Louisa suffers a severe head injury after jumping from the steps. Anne remains composed and helps organize the response.","summary":{"short":"At Lyme, the group encounters Mr. Elliot, and Louisa suffers a severe head injury after jumping from the steps. Anne remains composed and helps organize the response.","detailed":"During a morning walk in Lyme, an unknown gentleman admires Anne; the party later discovers that he is Mr. Elliot, Sir Walter's heir and Anne's estranged cousin. The group continues walking with the Harvilles and Benwick. Louisa insists that Wentworth jump her down from a set of steps, repeats the stunt, and falls badly, losing consciousness. While others panic, Anne gives practical directions and helps secure medical care. Louisa remains in Lyme under the Harvilles' care. Wentworth, shaken and guilty, increasingly respects Anne's judgment and asks her advice before returning toward Uppercross to inform the Musgroves."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch11_12","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 11 & 12","analysis":"The analysis identifies Louisa's fall as the novel's major mid-point climax and notes that the original two-volume publication ended its first volume at Chapter 12, giving the accident the effect of a cliffhanger. The event also revises Wentworth's earlier admiration for firmness of character: Louisa's refusal to be persuaded becomes dangerous stubbornness, causing serious injury and forcing Wentworth to reconsider whether unwavering resolution is always a virtue.","analysis_chapters":[11,12],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S47","chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P379","paragraph_end":"P379","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Lyme Cobb and shore","dataset_locations":["Lyme Cobb and shore"]},{"scene_id":"S48","chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P380","paragraph_end":"P408","paragraph_count":29,"location":"Lyme Regis","dataset_locations":["Lyme Regis"]},{"scene_id":"S49","chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P409","paragraph_end":"P409","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Lyme Cobb and shore","dataset_locations":["Lyme Cobb and shore"]},{"scene_id":"S50","chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P410","paragraph_end":"P410","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Lyme Regis","dataset_locations":["Lyme Regis"]},{"scene_id":"S51","chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P411","paragraph_end":"P411","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Lyme Cobb and shore","dataset_locations":["Lyme Cobb and shore"]},{"scene_id":"S52","chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P412","paragraph_end":"P426","paragraph_count":15,"location":"Lyme Regis","dataset_locations":["Lyme Regis"]},{"scene_id":"S53","chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P427","paragraph_end":"P428","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Lyme Cobb and shore","dataset_locations":["Lyme Cobb and shore"]},{"scene_id":"S54","chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P429","paragraph_end":"P447","paragraph_count":19,"location":"Lyme Regis","dataset_locations":["Lyme Regis"]},{"scene_id":"S55","chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P448","paragraph_end":"P448","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Lyme Cobb and shore","dataset_locations":["Lyme Cobb and shore"]},{"scene_id":"S56","chapter_id":"C12","paragraph_start":"P449","paragraph_end":"P455","paragraph_count":7,"location":"Lyme Regis","dataset_locations":["Lyme Regis"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section6/"}},{"chapter_id":"C13","chapter_number":13,"source_chapter_id":"ch13","paragraph_start":"P456","paragraph_end":"P487","paragraph_count":32,"scene_ids":["S57"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Uppercross and Kellynch"],"tooltip_summary":"As Louisa slowly recovers in Lyme, Anne leaves Uppercross to stay with Lady Russell and later visits the Crofts at Kellynch, where she hears that Wentworth praised her conduct during the accident.","summary":{"short":"As Louisa slowly recovers in Lyme, Anne leaves Uppercross to stay with Lady Russell and later visits the Crofts at Kellynch, where she hears that Wentworth praised her conduct during the accident.","detailed":"News of Louisa's gradual improvement reaches Uppercross while members of the Musgrove family rotate visits to Lyme. Anne moves from Uppercross to Lady Russell's company. Their reunion feels slightly strained because Anne's recent experiences have shifted her emotional priorities. Lady Russell and Anne visit Kellynch, now occupied by the Crofts, and Anne feels the pain of seeing others settled in her former home. Admiral and Mrs. Croft speak warmly of her and report Wentworth's admiration for her competence during the Lyme crisis. Anne is flattered, though the Crofts' upcoming absence makes another meeting with Wentworth less likely."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch13_14","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 13 & 14","analysis":"SparkNotes emphasizes contrasts among characters and households. Admiral Croft's simple tastes, including his amusement at Sir Walter's excessive mirrors, expose Sir Walter's vanity by contrast. Likewise, the lively, affectionate Musgrove household is set against the emotional coldness Anne expects in Bath. The chapters therefore use domestic spaces and everyday behavior to sharpen characterization and prepare for Anne's transition into the Bath portion of the novel.","analysis_chapters":[13,14],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S57","chapter_id":"C13","paragraph_start":"P456","paragraph_end":"P487","paragraph_count":32,"location":"Uppercross and Kellynch","dataset_locations":["Uppercross and Kellynch"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section7/"}},{"chapter_id":"C14","chapter_number":14,"source_chapter_id":"ch14","paragraph_start":"P488","paragraph_end":"P524","paragraph_count":37,"scene_ids":["S58","S59","S60","S61","S62","S63"],"scene_count":6,"dataset_locations":["Journey to Bath","Camden Place, Bath"],"tooltip_summary":"Mary and Charles return from Lyme with news that Louisa is improving and that Captain Benwick may be interested in Anne. Anne then prepares to travel with Lady Russell to Bath.","summary":{"short":"Mary and Charles return from Lyme with news that Louisa is improving and that Captain Benwick may be interested in Anne. Anne then prepares to travel with Lady Russell to Bath.","detailed":"Charles and Mary visit Anne and Lady Russell after returning from Lyme. Louisa is still weak but recovering, while Mary's account of her stay emphasizes her own pleasures more than nursing. Charles teasingly suggests that Captain Benwick has become interested in Anne and speaks highly of her; Mary dismisses the idea. A possible visit from Benwick never materializes. Meanwhile, letters from Bath report that Mr. Elliot has renewed contact with Sir Walter and Elizabeth. With the Uppercross and Lyme crisis subsiding, Anne and Lady Russell turn toward Bath, where Anne will rejoin her immediate family."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch13_14","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 13 & 14","analysis":"SparkNotes emphasizes contrasts among characters and households. Admiral Croft's simple tastes, including his amusement at Sir Walter's excessive mirrors, expose Sir Walter's vanity by contrast. Likewise, the lively, affectionate Musgrove household is set against the emotional coldness Anne expects in Bath. The chapters therefore use domestic spaces and everyday behavior to sharpen characterization and prepare for Anne's transition into the Bath portion of the novel.","analysis_chapters":[13,14],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S58","chapter_id":"C14","paragraph_start":"P488","paragraph_end":"P519","paragraph_count":32,"location":"Journey to Bath","dataset_locations":["Journey to Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S59","chapter_id":"C14","paragraph_start":"P520","paragraph_end":"P520","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S60","chapter_id":"C14","paragraph_start":"P521","paragraph_end":"P521","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Journey to Bath","dataset_locations":["Journey to Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S61","chapter_id":"C14","paragraph_start":"P522","paragraph_end":"P522","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S62","chapter_id":"C14","paragraph_start":"P523","paragraph_end":"P523","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Journey to Bath","dataset_locations":["Journey to Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S63","chapter_id":"C14","paragraph_start":"P524","paragraph_end":"P524","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section7/"}},{"chapter_id":"C15","chapter_number":15,"source_chapter_id":"ch15","paragraph_start":"P525","paragraph_end":"P548","paragraph_count":24,"scene_ids":["S64","S65","S66"],"scene_count":3,"dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath","White Hart Inn, Bath"],"tooltip_summary":"Anne arrives in Bath and finds Sir Walter and Elizabeth pleased with their life at Camden Place. She also meets Mr. Elliot again and notices his particular interest in her.","summary":{"short":"Anne arrives in Bath and finds Sir Walter and Elizabeth pleased with their life at Camden Place. She also meets Mr. Elliot again and notices his particular interest in her.","detailed":"At Camden Place, Anne receives a warmer welcome than expected but quickly sees that her father and Elizabeth are absorbed in Bath society, their home, and appearances rather than in her experiences. They are delighted to have reconciled with Mr. Elliot, whose wife has died and who now visits frequently. Anne remains uncertain about the motive behind his return to the family. When Mr. Elliot visits, he recognizes Anne from Lyme and pays her marked attention. She finds him polished, intelligent, and agreeable, although she continues to observe him cautiously rather than accepting the family's enthusiasm without question."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch15_16","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 15 & 16","analysis":"The analysis introduces 'place' as both geographic location and social position. Bath changes the Elliots' relative standing: people who dominate socially in Somersetshire may rank below others in the city. Anne rejects the idea that worth should depend simply on location, title, or wealth; she values accomplishment, manners, and genuine merit. SparkNotes nevertheless argues that Anne is not indifferent to class. Her pride differs from Sir Walter's because she wants social distinction to rest on substance rather than empty appearance.","analysis_chapters":[15,16],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S64","chapter_id":"C15","paragraph_start":"P525","paragraph_end":"P542","paragraph_count":18,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S65","chapter_id":"C15","paragraph_start":"P543","paragraph_end":"P543","paragraph_count":1,"location":"White Hart Inn, Bath","dataset_locations":["White Hart Inn, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S66","chapter_id":"C15","paragraph_start":"P544","paragraph_end":"P548","paragraph_count":5,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section8/"}},{"chapter_id":"C16","chapter_number":16,"source_chapter_id":"ch16","paragraph_start":"P549","paragraph_end":"P570","paragraph_count":22,"scene_ids":["S67"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"],"tooltip_summary":"Lady Russell is impressed by Mr. Elliot, but Anne remains suspicious of him. The Elliots also eagerly pursue renewed contact with their aristocratic cousins, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret.","summary":{"short":"Lady Russell is impressed by Mr. Elliot, but Anne remains suspicious of him. The Elliots also eagerly pursue renewed contact with their aristocratic cousins, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret.","detailed":"Mrs. Clay offers to leave Camden Place after Anne's arrival, but Sir Walter and Elizabeth insist that she stay, renewing Anne's concern that Mrs. Clay might attract Sir Walter. Lady Russell is disturbed by Mrs. Clay's position in the household but is strongly pleased with Mr. Elliot, whom she sees as sensible and correct. Anne, however, suspects that his renewed family interest may involve marriage or inheritance and does not fully trust his openness. Sir Walter and Elizabeth become excited when Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret arrive in Bath, valuing the social prestige of reconnecting with titled relatives far more than Anne does."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch15_16","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 15 & 16","analysis":"The analysis introduces 'place' as both geographic location and social position. Bath changes the Elliots' relative standing: people who dominate socially in Somersetshire may rank below others in the city. Anne rejects the idea that worth should depend simply on location, title, or wealth; she values accomplishment, manners, and genuine merit. SparkNotes nevertheless argues that Anne is not indifferent to class. Her pride differs from Sir Walter's because she wants social distinction to rest on substance rather than empty appearance.","analysis_chapters":[15,16],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S67","chapter_id":"C16","paragraph_start":"P549","paragraph_end":"P570","paragraph_count":22,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section8/"}},{"chapter_id":"C17","chapter_number":17,"source_chapter_id":"ch17","paragraph_start":"P571","paragraph_end":"P600","paragraph_count":30,"scene_ids":["S68","S69","S70","S71","S72","S73","S74","S75"],"scene_count":8,"dataset_locations":["Bath social rooms","Westgate Buildings, Bath","Camden Place, Bath"],"tooltip_summary":"Anne renews her friendship with the impoverished Mrs. Smith and prioritizes visiting her over aristocratic invitations. Lady Russell becomes convinced that Mr. Elliot intends to court Anne.","summary":{"short":"Anne renews her friendship with the impoverished Mrs. Smith and prioritizes visiting her over aristocratic invitations. Lady Russell becomes convinced that Mr. Elliot intends to court Anne.","detailed":"Anne learns that her former school friend Mrs. Smith is living in difficult circumstances in Bath after her husband's death, debt, and her own severe illness. Anne visits her and finds that poverty and disability have not destroyed Mrs. Smith's intelligence or good spirits. Sir Walter is appalled that Anne prefers such company to socially important acquaintances. At a dinner involving the Elliots' fashionable connections, Mr. Elliot speaks admiringly of Anne to Lady Russell. Lady Russell interprets this as courtship and imagines Anne eventually becoming Lady Elliot of Kellynch, but Anne remains unconvinced by Mr. Elliot's character."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch17_18","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 17 & 18","analysis":"SparkNotes focuses on irony and on the variety of marriages Austen presents. The engagement between Louisa and Benwick is intentionally surprising because their temperaments seem poorly matched; it suggests that many marriages arise from timing, need, and practical compatibility rather than an idealized perfect passion. Mrs. Smith's reduced circumstances provide another perspective on class and marriage, showing how vulnerable a woman's social position can be. Anne's continued friendship with her demonstrates independence from purely status-based judgments.","analysis_chapters":[17,18],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S68","chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P571","paragraph_end":"P574","paragraph_count":4,"location":"Bath social rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath social rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S69","chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P575","paragraph_end":"P575","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Westgate Buildings, Bath","dataset_locations":["Westgate Buildings, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S70","chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P576","paragraph_end":"P583","paragraph_count":8,"location":"Bath social rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath social rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S71","chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P584","paragraph_end":"P584","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S72","chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P585","paragraph_end":"P585","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Westgate Buildings, Bath","dataset_locations":["Westgate Buildings, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S73","chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P586","paragraph_end":"P588","paragraph_count":3,"location":"Bath social rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath social rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S74","chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P589","paragraph_end":"P589","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Westgate Buildings, Bath","dataset_locations":["Westgate Buildings, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S75","chapter_id":"C17","paragraph_start":"P590","paragraph_end":"P600","paragraph_count":11,"location":"Bath social rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath social rooms"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section9/"}},{"chapter_id":"C18","chapter_number":18,"source_chapter_id":"ch18","paragraph_start":"P601","paragraph_end":"P654","paragraph_count":54,"scene_ids":["S76","S77","S78"],"scene_count":3,"dataset_locations":["Milsom Street, Bath","Camden Place, Bath"],"tooltip_summary":"Anne learns that Louisa Musgrove is engaged to Captain Benwick, leaving Wentworth unattached. She then meets Admiral Croft in Bath and hears that Wentworth does not seem distressed by the match.","summary":{"short":"Anne learns that Louisa Musgrove is engaged to Captain Benwick, leaving Wentworth unattached. She then meets Admiral Croft in Bath and hears that Wentworth does not seem distressed by the match.","detailed":"A letter from Mary announces the surprising engagement of Louisa and Captain Benwick, who became close during Louisa's recovery in Lyme. Anne is pleased for them and also privately relieved that Louisa will not marry Wentworth. She recognizes that the engagement reopens possibilities she had tried not to hope for. The Crofts are now in Bath, and Anne encounters Admiral Croft while walking. He discusses the engagement openly and notes that he and Mrs. Croft had expected Louisa to marry Wentworth. According to the Admiral, Wentworth appears untroubled, encouraging Anne to wonder whether his feelings have changed."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch17_18","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 17 & 18","analysis":"SparkNotes focuses on irony and on the variety of marriages Austen presents. The engagement between Louisa and Benwick is intentionally surprising because their temperaments seem poorly matched; it suggests that many marriages arise from timing, need, and practical compatibility rather than an idealized perfect passion. Mrs. Smith's reduced circumstances provide another perspective on class and marriage, showing how vulnerable a woman's social position can be. Anne's continued friendship with her demonstrates independence from purely status-based judgments.","analysis_chapters":[17,18],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S76","chapter_id":"C18","paragraph_start":"P601","paragraph_end":"P629","paragraph_count":29,"location":"Milsom Street, Bath","dataset_locations":["Milsom Street, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S77","chapter_id":"C18","paragraph_start":"P630","paragraph_end":"P630","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S78","chapter_id":"C18","paragraph_start":"P631","paragraph_end":"P654","paragraph_count":24,"location":"Milsom Street, Bath","dataset_locations":["Milsom Street, Bath"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section9/"}},{"chapter_id":"C19","chapter_number":19,"source_chapter_id":"ch19","paragraph_start":"P655","paragraph_end":"P690","paragraph_count":36,"scene_ids":["S79","S80","S81","S82","S83","S84","S85","S86","S87","S88","S89","S90","S91","S92","S93"],"scene_count":15,"dataset_locations":["Bath streets","Milsom Street, Bath","Camden Place, Bath","Union Street, Bath","Bath shops","Bath concert rooms"],"tooltip_summary":"Wentworth arrives in Bath and unexpectedly meets Anne in town. His reaction to seeing her with Mr. Elliot suggests jealousy, while Anne eagerly anticipates another meeting at a concert.","summary":{"short":"Wentworth arrives in Bath and unexpectedly meets Anne in town. His reaction to seeing her with Mr. Elliot suggests jealousy, while Anne eagerly anticipates another meeting at a concert.","detailed":"Anne is walking in Bath with Elizabeth, Mrs. Clay, and Mr. Elliot when rain forces the party into a shop. Captain Wentworth appears and is visibly surprised to see Anne. Elizabeth barely acknowledges him because she still considers him socially beneath the family. When carriage space is limited, Wentworth offers Anne help and his umbrella, but Mr. Elliot returns and escorts her away, creating the appearance of intimacy between the cousins. The next day Lady Russell appears to ignore Wentworth in the street. Anne, increasingly frustrated by Bath's formal society, looks forward to a public concert where she expects to see him again."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch19_20","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 19 & 20","analysis":"The analysis treats misunderstanding and bad timing as the main obstacles between Anne and Wentworth. Mr. Elliot's presence makes each uncertain of the other's feelings and heightens the tension as the novel approaches its climax. SparkNotes also emphasizes the characters' emotional restraint: their love is not expressed through public declarations but unfolds gradually within the rules of civility. This restraint reflects Austen's preference for passion tempered by prudence and social consideration.","analysis_chapters":[19,20],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S79","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P655","paragraph_end":"P655","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath streets","dataset_locations":["Bath streets"]},{"scene_id":"S80","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P656","paragraph_end":"P656","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Milsom Street, Bath","dataset_locations":["Milsom Street, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S81","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P657","paragraph_end":"P657","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S82","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P658","paragraph_end":"P658","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath streets","dataset_locations":["Bath streets"]},{"scene_id":"S83","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P659","paragraph_end":"P659","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Union Street, Bath","dataset_locations":["Union Street, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S84","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P660","paragraph_end":"P660","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Milsom Street, Bath","dataset_locations":["Milsom Street, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S85","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P661","paragraph_end":"P663","paragraph_count":3,"location":"Bath streets","dataset_locations":["Bath streets"]},{"scene_id":"S86","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P664","paragraph_end":"P664","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath shops","dataset_locations":["Bath shops"]},{"scene_id":"S87","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P665","paragraph_end":"P677","paragraph_count":13,"location":"Bath streets","dataset_locations":["Bath streets"]},{"scene_id":"S88","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P678","paragraph_end":"P678","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S89","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P679","paragraph_end":"P685","paragraph_count":7,"location":"Bath streets","dataset_locations":["Bath streets"]},{"scene_id":"S90","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P686","paragraph_end":"P686","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath concert rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S91","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P687","paragraph_end":"P688","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Bath streets","dataset_locations":["Bath streets"]},{"scene_id":"S92","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P689","paragraph_end":"P689","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath concert rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S93","chapter_id":"C19","paragraph_start":"P690","paragraph_end":"P690","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath streets","dataset_locations":["Bath streets"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section10/"}},{"chapter_id":"C20","chapter_number":20,"source_chapter_id":"ch20","paragraph_start":"P691","paragraph_end":"P741","paragraph_count":51,"scene_ids":["S94","S95","S96"],"scene_count":3,"dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms","Camden Place, Bath"],"tooltip_summary":"At the Bath concert, Anne and Wentworth have a meaningful conversation, but Mr. Elliot monopolizes Anne afterward. Wentworth becomes jealous and leaves before Anne can explain herself.","summary":{"short":"At the Bath concert, Anne and Wentworth have a meaningful conversation, but Mr. Elliot monopolizes Anne afterward. Wentworth becomes jealous and leaves before Anne can explain herself.","detailed":"At the concert, Sir Walter and Elizabeth finally acknowledge Wentworth more civilly, reflecting his improved wealth and status. Wentworth speaks with Anne about Louisa and Benwick, praising Anne's calm behavior at Lyme and questioning whether the new couple are truly well matched. Anne is encouraged by his warmth. During the performance, however, Mr. Elliot sits beside her, flatters her, and hints strongly at marriage. Anne repeatedly tries to reconnect with Wentworth, but the social arrangement keeps them apart. Seeing Mr. Elliot's attention and misunderstanding Anne's feelings, Wentworth grows jealous, says farewell, and leaves the concert."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch19_20","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 19 & 20","analysis":"The analysis treats misunderstanding and bad timing as the main obstacles between Anne and Wentworth. Mr. Elliot's presence makes each uncertain of the other's feelings and heightens the tension as the novel approaches its climax. SparkNotes also emphasizes the characters' emotional restraint: their love is not expressed through public declarations but unfolds gradually within the rules of civility. This restraint reflects Austen's preference for passion tempered by prudence and social consideration.","analysis_chapters":[19,20],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S94","chapter_id":"C20","paragraph_start":"P691","paragraph_end":"P717","paragraph_count":27,"location":"Bath concert rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S95","chapter_id":"C20","paragraph_start":"P718","paragraph_end":"P718","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S96","chapter_id":"C20","paragraph_start":"P719","paragraph_end":"P741","paragraph_count":23,"location":"Bath concert rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section10/"}},{"chapter_id":"C21","chapter_number":21,"source_chapter_id":"ch21","paragraph_start":"P742","paragraph_end":"P845","paragraph_count":104,"scene_ids":["S97","S98","S99","S100","S101","S102","S103","S104","S105","S106","S107","S108","S109"],"scene_count":13,"dataset_locations":["Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings","Camden Place, Bath","Bath concert rooms"],"tooltip_summary":"Mrs. Smith reveals Mr. Elliot's past selfishness and his real motives for returning to the Elliot family. Anne realizes he is manipulating the family to protect his inheritance.","summary":{"short":"Mrs. Smith reveals Mr. Elliot's past selfishness and his real motives for returning to the Elliot family. Anne realizes he is manipulating the family to protect his inheritance.","detailed":"Anne visits Mrs. Smith, who initially assumes that Anne plans to marry Mr. Elliot. Once Anne denies this, Mrs. Smith explains her history with him. Mr. Elliot had encouraged the Smiths' extravagant lifestyle, abandoned them after Mr. Smith's death, and previously expressed contempt for the Elliot title and estate. His recent reconciliation with Sir Walter is strategic: he fears Sir Walter may marry Mrs. Clay and produce a son who would displace him as heir. His interest in Anne adds another motive, but his plans remain tied to controlling the succession. Anne is shocked and resolves to warn Lady Russell and protect her family."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch21_22","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 21 & 22","analysis":"SparkNotes frames these chapters around the exposure of Mr. Elliot's deception. Mrs. Smith, whom polite society marginalizes because of poverty and illness, becomes the person who gives Anne the knowledge needed to see through a wealthy man's manipulation. This reversal creates a form of social justice. The analysis distinguishes acceptable social ambition from corrupt ambition: considering fortune or rank may be prudent, but lying, feigning affection, and manipulating others for a title violate the conduct expected of a gentleman.","analysis_chapters":[21,22],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S97","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P742","paragraph_end":"P743","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings","dataset_locations":["Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings"]},{"scene_id":"S98","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P744","paragraph_end":"P744","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S99","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P745","paragraph_end":"P745","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings","dataset_locations":["Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings"]},{"scene_id":"S100","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P746","paragraph_end":"P747","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Bath concert rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S101","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P748","paragraph_end":"P754","paragraph_count":7,"location":"Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings","dataset_locations":["Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings"]},{"scene_id":"S102","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P755","paragraph_end":"P755","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath concert rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S103","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P756","paragraph_end":"P773","paragraph_count":18,"location":"Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings","dataset_locations":["Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings"]},{"scene_id":"S104","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P774","paragraph_end":"P774","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath concert rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S105","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P775","paragraph_end":"P829","paragraph_count":55,"location":"Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings","dataset_locations":["Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings"]},{"scene_id":"S106","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P830","paragraph_end":"P830","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S107","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P831","paragraph_end":"P831","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings","dataset_locations":["Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings"]},{"scene_id":"S108","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P832","paragraph_end":"P832","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S109","chapter_id":"C21","paragraph_start":"P833","paragraph_end":"P845","paragraph_count":13,"location":"Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings","dataset_locations":["Mrs Smith's rooms, Westgate Buildings"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section11/"}},{"chapter_id":"C22","chapter_number":22,"source_chapter_id":"ch22","paragraph_start":"P846","paragraph_end":"P915","paragraph_count":70,"scene_ids":["S110","S111","S112","S113","S114","S115","S116","S117","S118","S119","S120","S121","S122","S123","S124","S125","S126","S127","S128"],"scene_count":19,"dataset_locations":["Bath","Camden Place, Bath","Bath concert rooms","White Hart Inn, Bath","Pump Room, Bath"],"tooltip_summary":"The Musgroves arrive in Bath for Henrietta's wedding preparations. Anne sees Mr. Elliot speaking privately with Mrs. Clay and becomes absorbed again in the warmer Musgrove social circle.","summary":{"short":"The Musgroves arrive in Bath for Henrietta's wedding preparations. Anne sees Mr. Elliot speaking privately with Mrs. Clay and becomes absorbed again in the warmer Musgrove social circle.","detailed":"Mr. Elliot notices Anne's sudden coldness but does not know that she has learned the truth about him. Before Anne can report everything to Lady Russell, Charles and Mary unexpectedly arrive, followed by Mrs. Musgrove, Henrietta, and Captain Harville. Henrietta is preparing to marry Charles Hayter, and Anne warmly approves of the Musgroves' willingness to value happiness over status. Visiting their lodgings restores Anne to a more natural and affectionate social environment than the Elliots' Bath circle. From a window, Mary and Anne see Mr. Elliot speaking with Mrs. Clay, confirming that the two have a connection concealed from Sir Walter and Elizabeth."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch21_22","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 21 & 22","analysis":"SparkNotes frames these chapters around the exposure of Mr. Elliot's deception. Mrs. Smith, whom polite society marginalizes because of poverty and illness, becomes the person who gives Anne the knowledge needed to see through a wealthy man's manipulation. This reversal creates a form of social justice. The analysis distinguishes acceptable social ambition from corrupt ambition: considering fortune or rank may be prudent, but lying, feigning affection, and manipulating others for a title violate the conduct expected of a gentleman.","analysis_chapters":[21,22],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S110","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P846","paragraph_end":"P858","paragraph_count":13,"location":"Bath","dataset_locations":["Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S111","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P859","paragraph_end":"P859","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S112","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P860","paragraph_end":"P860","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath","dataset_locations":["Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S113","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P861","paragraph_end":"P861","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath concert rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S114","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P862","paragraph_end":"P863","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Bath","dataset_locations":["Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S115","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P864","paragraph_end":"P864","paragraph_count":1,"location":"White Hart Inn, Bath","dataset_locations":["White Hart Inn, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S116","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P865","paragraph_end":"P873","paragraph_count":9,"location":"Bath","dataset_locations":["Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S117","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P874","paragraph_end":"P874","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S118","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P875","paragraph_end":"P875","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath","dataset_locations":["Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S119","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P876","paragraph_end":"P876","paragraph_count":1,"location":"White Hart Inn, Bath","dataset_locations":["White Hart Inn, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S120","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P877","paragraph_end":"P877","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Pump Room, Bath","dataset_locations":["Pump Room, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S121","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P878","paragraph_end":"P878","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath concert rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S122","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P879","paragraph_end":"P888","paragraph_count":10,"location":"Bath","dataset_locations":["Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S123","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P889","paragraph_end":"P889","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S124","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P890","paragraph_end":"P894","paragraph_count":5,"location":"Bath","dataset_locations":["Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S125","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P895","paragraph_end":"P895","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S126","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P896","paragraph_end":"P912","paragraph_count":17,"location":"Bath","dataset_locations":["Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S127","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P913","paragraph_end":"P913","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S128","chapter_id":"C22","paragraph_start":"P914","paragraph_end":"P915","paragraph_count":2,"location":"Bath","dataset_locations":["Bath"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section11/"}},{"chapter_id":"C23","chapter_number":23,"source_chapter_id":"ch23","paragraph_start":"P916","paragraph_end":"P998","paragraph_count":83,"scene_ids":["S129","S130","S131","S132","S133","S134","S135","S136","S137","S138"],"scene_count":10,"dataset_locations":["White Hart Inn, Bath","Camden Place, Bath","Union Street, Bath","Bath concert rooms","Milsom Street, Bath"],"tooltip_summary":"Wentworth overhears Anne arguing that women can love faithfully for years, then secretly gives her a letter declaring that he still loves her. They reconcile and agree to marry.","summary":{"short":"Wentworth overhears Anne arguing that women can love faithfully for years, then secretly gives her a letter declaring that he still loves her. They reconcile and agree to marry.","detailed":"Anne spends the day with the Musgroves, Captain Harville, Mrs. Croft, and Wentworth. In a parlor, Anne and Harville debate whether men or women remain more constant in love; Anne argues that women may continue loving even after hope is gone. Wentworth, writing nearby, hears the conversation and responds by slipping Anne a letter confessing that he still loves her. Overcome, Anne leaves and soon meets Wentworth in the street, where they finally speak openly and confirm their mutual love. Later, they revisit their eight-year separation: Anne defends following Lady Russell's advice, while Wentworth admits that pride kept him from returning sooner."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch23_24","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 23 & 24","analysis":"The final analysis connects Anne and Wentworth's marriage to social mobility. Wentworth's naval career has given him enough wealth and standing to become an acceptable match for a baronet's daughter, while Sir Walter's debts have diminished the practical importance of the Elliot family's rank. SparkNotes argues that Anne's eventual happiness does not require her to reject duty entirely; rather, the social circumstances themselves have changed. The Navy therefore becomes both a source of national service and a mechanism through which the social order can become more flexible.","analysis_chapters":[23,24],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S129","chapter_id":"C23","paragraph_start":"P916","paragraph_end":"P968","paragraph_count":53,"location":"White Hart Inn, Bath","dataset_locations":["White Hart Inn, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S130","chapter_id":"C23","paragraph_start":"P969","paragraph_end":"P969","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S131","chapter_id":"C23","paragraph_start":"P970","paragraph_end":"P970","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Union Street, Bath","dataset_locations":["Union Street, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S132","chapter_id":"C23","paragraph_start":"P971","paragraph_end":"P972","paragraph_count":2,"location":"White Hart Inn, Bath","dataset_locations":["White Hart Inn, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S133","chapter_id":"C23","paragraph_start":"P973","paragraph_end":"P973","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Camden Place, Bath","dataset_locations":["Camden Place, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S134","chapter_id":"C23","paragraph_start":"P974","paragraph_end":"P974","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Union Street, Bath","dataset_locations":["Union Street, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S135","chapter_id":"C23","paragraph_start":"P975","paragraph_end":"P975","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Bath concert rooms","dataset_locations":["Bath concert rooms"]},{"scene_id":"S136","chapter_id":"C23","paragraph_start":"P976","paragraph_end":"P984","paragraph_count":9,"location":"White Hart Inn, Bath","dataset_locations":["White Hart Inn, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S137","chapter_id":"C23","paragraph_start":"P985","paragraph_end":"P985","paragraph_count":1,"location":"Milsom Street, Bath","dataset_locations":["Milsom Street, Bath"]},{"scene_id":"S138","chapter_id":"C23","paragraph_start":"P986","paragraph_end":"P998","paragraph_count":13,"location":"White Hart Inn, Bath","dataset_locations":["White Hart Inn, Bath"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section12/"}},{"chapter_id":"C24","chapter_number":24,"source_chapter_id":"ch24","paragraph_start":"P999","paragraph_end":"P1011","paragraph_count":13,"scene_ids":["S139"],"scene_count":1,"dataset_locations":["Bath aftermath"],"tooltip_summary":"Anne and Wentworth announce their engagement and marry. The family gradually accepts the match, while Mr. Elliot leaves Bath and Mrs. Clay becomes associated with him.","summary":{"short":"Anne and Wentworth announce their engagement and marry. The family gradually accepts the match, while Mr. Elliot leaves Bath and Mrs. Clay becomes associated with him.","detailed":"The narrator closes the novel by resolving the principal relationships. Wentworth's fortune now makes him acceptable even to Sir Walter, and neither Sir Walter nor Elizabeth openly opposes the engagement. Lady Russell initially regrets her earlier judgment but ultimately embraces Anne's happiness and grows to appreciate Wentworth. Mr. Elliot withdraws from Bath after his plans fail, while Mrs. Clay leaves the Elliot household and is rumored to be under his protection. Wentworth helps Mrs. Smith recover part of her late husband's property. Anne gains a loving marriage and a place within the naval community she has come to value."},"analysis_id":"analysis_ch23_24","analysis_label":"Analysis: Chapters 23 & 24","analysis":"The final analysis connects Anne and Wentworth's marriage to social mobility. Wentworth's naval career has given him enough wealth and standing to become an acceptable match for a baronet's daughter, while Sir Walter's debts have diminished the practical importance of the Elliot family's rank. SparkNotes argues that Anne's eventual happiness does not require her to reject duty entirely; rather, the social circumstances themselves have changed. The Navy therefore becomes both a source of national service and a mechanism through which the social order can become more flexible.","analysis_chapters":[23,24],"scene_alignment":[{"scene_id":"S139","chapter_id":"C24","paragraph_start":"P999","paragraph_end":"P1011","paragraph_count":13,"location":"Bath aftermath","dataset_locations":["Bath aftermath"]}],"source":{"sparklet_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/sparklets/","detailed_summary_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section12/"}}],"analysis_groups":[{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch01_02","chapters":[1,2],"chapter_ids":["C01","C02"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 1 & 2","analysis":"SparkNotes reads the opening chapters as establishing both the Elliot family's financial crisis and the social pressures behind it. Sir Walter's need to preserve the appearance of aristocratic status makes retrenchment difficult, while the decision to move to Bath demonstrates persuasion working through his vanity rather than through practical reasoning. The analysis also treats Sir Walter as a foil to Anne: his vanity, image-consciousness, and poor judgment highlight her humility, sensibility, and practical intelligence.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section1/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch03_04","chapters":[3,4],"chapter_ids":["C03","C04"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 3 & 4","analysis":"The analysis emphasizes the Navy as a vehicle of social mobility that conflicts with Sir Walter's attachment to inherited rank. Anne values naval service, while Sir Walter resents the ability of officers of obscure birth to gain wealth and status. Chapter 4 develops the novel's central concern with persuasion through Anne's broken engagement: she yields to older advisers despite her own feelings, leaving open the question of when persuadability is prudent and when it becomes a weakness. SparkNotes also notes Austen's use of free indirect discourse to blend a character's viewpoint with the narrator's voice.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section2/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch05_06","chapters":[5,6],"chapter_ids":["C05","C06"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 5 & 6","analysis":"SparkNotes uses Anne's movement between the Elliot and Musgrove households to examine class distinctions. The Musgroves are wealthy landowners but untitled, and their domestic interests contrast with the Elliots' fixation on rank and appearance. Anne appreciates their warmth without idealizing their lack of education or elegance. The analysis also focuses on women's precarious social position within the class system: marriage can rapidly raise or lower a woman's status, which helps explain the family's anxiety about Mrs. Clay and the importance placed on choosing a socially appropriate husband.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section3/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch07_08","chapters":[7,8],"chapter_ids":["C07","C08"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 7 & 8","analysis":"The analysis highlights Austen's free indirect discourse, especially as the narration tracks Anne's intense response to seeing Wentworth again without simply quoting her thoughts. It also compares different performances of motherhood. Mary's concern for her injured son is presented as shallow and self-centered, whereas Mrs. Musgrove's grief for her late son is more genuine, though it too creates a social claim on Wentworth's attention. These contrasts show Austen's interest in how people inhabit family and social roles.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section4/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch09_10","chapters":[9,10],"chapter_ids":["C09","C10"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 9 & 10","analysis":"SparkNotes treats Admiral and Mrs. Croft as one of Austen's clearest models of a successful long-term marriage. Their shared handling of the carriage becomes a metaphor for their partnership: responsibility shifts naturally between them as circumstances require. The analysis presents this relationship as unusually egalitarian for its historical context, challenging rigid divisions between male and female spheres and offering a model of the kind of companionate marriage the novel values.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section5/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch11_12","chapters":[11,12],"chapter_ids":["C11","C12"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 11 & 12","analysis":"The analysis identifies Louisa's fall as the novel's major mid-point climax and notes that the original two-volume publication ended its first volume at Chapter 12, giving the accident the effect of a cliffhanger. The event also revises Wentworth's earlier admiration for firmness of character: Louisa's refusal to be persuaded becomes dangerous stubbornness, causing serious injury and forcing Wentworth to reconsider whether unwavering resolution is always a virtue.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section6/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch13_14","chapters":[13,14],"chapter_ids":["C13","C14"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 13 & 14","analysis":"SparkNotes emphasizes contrasts among characters and households. Admiral Croft's simple tastes, including his amusement at Sir Walter's excessive mirrors, expose Sir Walter's vanity by contrast. Likewise, the lively, affectionate Musgrove household is set against the emotional coldness Anne expects in Bath. The chapters therefore use domestic spaces and everyday behavior to sharpen characterization and prepare for Anne's transition into the Bath portion of the novel.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section7/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch15_16","chapters":[15,16],"chapter_ids":["C15","C16"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 15 & 16","analysis":"The analysis introduces 'place' as both geographic location and social position. Bath changes the Elliots' relative standing: people who dominate socially in Somersetshire may rank below others in the city. Anne rejects the idea that worth should depend simply on location, title, or wealth; she values accomplishment, manners, and genuine merit. SparkNotes nevertheless argues that Anne is not indifferent to class. Her pride differs from Sir Walter's because she wants social distinction to rest on substance rather than empty appearance.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section8/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch17_18","chapters":[17,18],"chapter_ids":["C17","C18"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 17 & 18","analysis":"SparkNotes focuses on irony and on the variety of marriages Austen presents. The engagement between Louisa and Benwick is intentionally surprising because their temperaments seem poorly matched; it suggests that many marriages arise from timing, need, and practical compatibility rather than an idealized perfect passion. Mrs. Smith's reduced circumstances provide another perspective on class and marriage, showing how vulnerable a woman's social position can be. Anne's continued friendship with her demonstrates independence from purely status-based judgments.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section9/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch19_20","chapters":[19,20],"chapter_ids":["C19","C20"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 19 & 20","analysis":"The analysis treats misunderstanding and bad timing as the main obstacles between Anne and Wentworth. Mr. Elliot's presence makes each uncertain of the other's feelings and heightens the tension as the novel approaches its climax. SparkNotes also emphasizes the characters' emotional restraint: their love is not expressed through public declarations but unfolds gradually within the rules of civility. This restraint reflects Austen's preference for passion tempered by prudence and social consideration.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section10/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch21_22","chapters":[21,22],"chapter_ids":["C21","C22"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 21 & 22","analysis":"SparkNotes frames these chapters around the exposure of Mr. Elliot's deception. Mrs. Smith, whom polite society marginalizes because of poverty and illness, becomes the person who gives Anne the knowledge needed to see through a wealthy man's manipulation. This reversal creates a form of social justice. The analysis distinguishes acceptable social ambition from corrupt ambition: considering fortune or rank may be prudent, but lying, feigning affection, and manipulating others for a title violate the conduct expected of a gentleman.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section11/"}},{"analysis_id":"analysis_ch23_24","chapters":[23,24],"chapter_ids":["C23","C24"],"label":"Analysis: Chapters 23 & 24","analysis":"The final analysis connects Anne and Wentworth's marriage to social mobility. Wentworth's naval career has given him enough wealth and standing to become an acceptable match for a baronet's daughter, while Sir Walter's debts have diminished the practical importance of the Elliot family's rank. SparkNotes argues that Anne's eventual happiness does not require her to reject duty entirely; rather, the social circumstances themselves have changed. The Navy therefore becomes both a source of national service and a mechanism through which the social order can become more flexible.","source":{"summary_and_analysis_page":"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/persuasion/section12/"}}]}`);export{a as default};
